WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:09.010
- Good afternoon and good evening,

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:11.170
depending on your location,

00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:12.210
I'm Saad Omer,

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:15.500
the Director of the Yale
Institute for Global Health.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.560
I'm really delighted to welcome you

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:20.250
to the 'Global Health Conversation Series'

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:21.660
sponsored by and led by

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:23.130
the Yale Institute for Global Health

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:25.170
and as a lot of you know,

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:27.193
already that the series is a venue

00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:31.050
for the Yale community
and beyond to learn from

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:35.013
and connect with innovative
leaders in global health.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:40.100
In addition to YIGH, we are
grateful for the Walker Fund

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:42.120
by the McMillan Center at Yale

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:46.540
for co-sponsoring the series
including today's event,

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:49.880
it's a pleasure to be all of you today.

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:53.240
As you know the world faces
unprecedented challenges

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:58.000
of humanitarian crises in
more than 2000 countries

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.042
resulting from armed
conflicts, natural disasters.

00:01:02.042 --> 00:01:05.210
These are being accelerated and multiplied

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:06.800
by the global climate change

00:01:09.078 --> 00:01:12.203
and the ongoing COVID-19 global pandemic.

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:17.310
These emergencies have significant
impact on global health,

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:21.313
including increased death
illness and disability.

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:24.490
In addition to that, the
economic cost of course,

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:28.090
of these crises are enormous.

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:33.080
So, found at the call of
Albert Einstein in 1933,

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:36.610
the International Rescue Committee,

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:38.700
so I guess no pressure on their leaders

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:40.823
to stand up to this legacy.

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:44.400
And so International
Rescue Committee's mission

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:47.810
is to help the world's
most vulnerable people

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:51.670
whose lives and livelihoods
have been shattered

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:54.810
by conflict and disaster,
including the climate crisis

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:59.140
and to help them survive,
recover and regain control

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:00.053
of their future.

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:06.590
And so IRC delivers lasting
impact by providing healthcare

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:11.320
and engaging in activities
that help children learn

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:14.280
and empower individuals
to become self reliant

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.650
in these kinds of situations.

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:21.230
And so to discuss how we
sustain health and wellbeing

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:24.010
in the face of disaster,

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:26.360
we are pleased to have with us today

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:31.360
David Miliband the
President and CEO of IRC.

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:36.230
Since 2013, Mr. Miliband has overseen

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:42.450
the agency's operations in
40 crisis conflict countries

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:45.920
and its refugees resettlement
and in assistance program

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:48.740
throughout Europe and the Americas.

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:50.440
Prior to joining the IRC,

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:54.750
he has had a distinguished
political career in the UK

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:58.110
from 2007 to 2010 for example,

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:01.080
he served as the 74th Secretary of State

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:04.043
for Foreign and Commonwealth Affairs,

00:03:06.109 --> 00:03:10.020
in short, often referred to
as the UK Foreign Secretary,

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:11.890
if I'm not mischaracterizing it

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:17.330
and representing the UK
throughout the world.

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:20.245
His accomplishments have
earned him a reputation

00:03:20.245 --> 00:03:22.473
in former President Bill Clinton's words,

00:03:23.538 --> 00:03:26.240
"As one of the ablest most creative

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:27.967
public servants of our time."

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:33.950
And in 2016, Mr. Milliband was named

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:35.590
one of the World's Greatest Leaders

00:03:35.590 --> 00:03:37.060
by 'Fortune Magazine'

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:39.490
and in 2018, he was inducted

00:03:39.490 --> 00:03:42.330
to the American Academy
of Arts and Sciences

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:45.060
and those of you who
have gone to that place,

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:47.300
it's quite a legacy to be inducted

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:50.000
in the American Academy
of Arts and Sciences,

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:53.580
it has acceptance letters
from Charles Darwin,

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:58.580
I think Thomas Jefferson himself
if I'm not misremembering,

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:02.567
but at least a few of those individuals

00:04:02.567 --> 00:04:06.780
who have contributed to
this country and beyond

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:12.250
in meaningful ways, so
he's in good company there.

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:14.840
He's an author of the book,

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:18.100
'Rescue: Refugees and the
Political Crisis of Our Time.'

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:19.593
As a son of refugees,

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:24.714
David, if I may call
you by your first name

00:04:24.714 --> 00:04:27.853
brings a personal
commitment to the IRCs work

00:04:27.853 --> 00:04:30.060
and to the premise of the book

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:33.252
that we can rescue the
dignity and hopes of refugees

00:04:33.252 --> 00:04:34.580
and displaced people

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:36.770
and if we help them in the process,

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:40.030
we will rescue rescue our own values.

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:42.855
Welcome David and it's a
pleasure to have you here.

00:04:42.855 --> 00:04:44.120
- Thanks very much, indeed,

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:46.920
it's really good to be with you

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:49.763
and I'm looking forward
to the conversation

00:04:49.763 --> 00:04:51.660
and the questions.

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:53.820
- Wonderful, so just to prime the pump

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:56.840
and give you an opportunity
to sort of broadly outline

00:04:56.840 --> 00:04:57.673
some of the things.

00:04:57.673 --> 00:05:02.427
So what is the scope and scale
of the humanitarian crises

00:05:04.290 --> 00:05:08.654
we are grappling with
around the globe right now?

00:05:08.654 --> 00:05:13.150
As I said, the IRC was founded
by Albert Einstein in 1933,

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:17.130
how does this era compare to other eras

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:18.413
in the history of IRC?

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:21.810
- Thanks very much for having me speak

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:24.950
to this distinguished group
and distinguished audience.

00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:26.220
Look, we've just published

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:29.140
our emergency watch list for 2022

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:32.970
and this covers the 20
countries that we think

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:33.910
will be the epicenter

00:05:33.910 --> 00:05:37.200
of humanitarian crisis for the year ahead.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:39.680
The population of these 20 countries

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:41.877
is about 800 million people,

00:05:41.877 --> 00:05:45.170
in other words, 10% of
the global population,

00:05:45.170 --> 00:05:50.170
but these 20 countries account
for about 80% of the people,

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:53.170
90% of the people in humanitarian need,

00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:55.570
including 80 or 90% of
the number of refugees,

00:05:55.570 --> 00:05:58.790
internally displaced,
civilians killed in conflict.

00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:00.390
So, in those 20 countries,

00:06:00.390 --> 00:06:03.060
there are about 275 million people

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:06.460
who are in need of humanitarian aid

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:08.120
led by a country like Afghanistan,

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:10.500
where 22 million people
are currently dependent

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:14.210
on the World Food Program
for their next meal.

00:06:14.210 --> 00:06:18.428
And these are countries
marked by a number of factors,

00:06:18.428 --> 00:06:20.820
many of which are different from before,

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:23.630
so obviously Einstein set up the IRC

00:06:23.630 --> 00:06:25.959
in advance of the Second World War.

00:06:25.959 --> 00:06:29.620
We now see that refugees
and humanitarian need

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:32.960
is generally, we obviously
all holding our breath

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:34.640
about the Russia-Ukraine situation,

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:37.510
but generally we are dealing
with wars within the states,

00:06:37.510 --> 00:06:38.343
not wars between states,

00:06:38.343 --> 00:06:41.370
there are actually no hot wars
between states at the moment

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:44.600
pending what happens
in Ukraine and Russia.

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:46.260
There are 55 civil wars going on,

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:48.430
so-called civil wars
going on at the moment,

00:06:48.430 --> 00:06:50.070
eight of them classified as severe

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:52.899
because they have more than a
thousand battlefield deaths.

00:06:52.899 --> 00:06:55.712
Afghanistan, as I said, top of the list

00:06:55.712 --> 00:06:58.560
for the war that's finished there,

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:03.560
but Ethiopia, Yemen high on the list.

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:06.010
The second point that's different

00:07:06.010 --> 00:07:09.010
from certainly the origins of the IRC

00:07:09.010 --> 00:07:12.870
is that the most likely
consequence of a civil war

00:07:12.870 --> 00:07:14.540
is a further war.

00:07:14.540 --> 00:07:17.080
The duration of conflict is extending,

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:22.080
the tools of diplomacy are weak
for dealing with civil wars,

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:26.020
the tools of diplomacy were
developed for negotiations

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:30.550
between states, not for
resolving war within states

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:33.400
and the duration of displacement
of need is becoming chronic

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:38.400
rather than just acute to
use health terminology.

00:07:38.530 --> 00:07:40.680
I suppose the third
element that's striking

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:43.600
about the numbers of people in need

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:46.320
is that half of them
are under the age of 18,

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:47.980
two-thirds of them are women and girls,

00:07:47.980 --> 00:07:52.320
so there's a strong gender
dimension to the structure

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:56.670
and geography of poverty
and of humanitarian need,

00:07:56.670 --> 00:08:00.520
so I think that's
important to flag as well.

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:04.330
Now these factors lead us
in our emergency watch list

00:08:04.330 --> 00:08:06.320
to say that there's
system failure going on,

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:08.070
states are failing in
their responsibilities

00:08:08.070 --> 00:08:09.980
to their own citizens,
diplomacy is failing,

00:08:09.980 --> 00:08:12.430
'cause there are 55 civil war still going.

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:17.310
The legal regime is failing
because rights to life

00:08:17.310 --> 00:08:21.340
and livelihood are being circumvented,

00:08:21.340 --> 00:08:22.770
impunity is the order of the day

00:08:22.770 --> 00:08:25.310
and the humanitarian aid system
is struggling to catch up.

00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:27.160
So that's the picture
that we are dealing with,

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:29.380
that's why we are now a
billion dollar organization

00:08:29.380 --> 00:08:34.380
with probably about 35,
37,000 staff of employees

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:39.121
and day workers around the world,

00:08:39.121 --> 00:08:43.010
why we've hired another
thousand people in Afghanistan

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:44.340
in the last three months.

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:47.528
And just to round out the picture,

00:08:47.528 --> 00:08:48.890
we're a $1 billion organization

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:52.793
in a 40, 41 billion dollar
sector of the global economy,

00:08:54.330 --> 00:08:56.840
which is called the
Humanitarian Aid sector

00:08:56.840 --> 00:09:01.130
and as we may discuss sits
in rather separate stovepipe

00:09:01.130 --> 00:09:03.500
from the development sector

00:09:03.500 --> 00:09:08.280
where much global health
provision has made progress

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:10.240
in the last 20 years

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:14.410
and where some of the
problems that are most acute

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:18.810
can be squarely seen in the
failure of these two systems

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:19.790
to work with each other.

00:09:19.790 --> 00:09:21.690
So I hope that gives you a picture.

00:09:21.690 --> 00:09:25.310
- No, that's interesting to say the least,

00:09:27.980 --> 00:09:31.475
the fact that the need is such...

00:09:31.475 --> 00:09:34.500
That the sector is active and growing,

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:39.500
but also the whole that
there is this safety net

00:09:44.030 --> 00:09:46.940
to mitigate the impact
of the system failure

00:09:46.940 --> 00:09:48.370
that you described.

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:50.650
So, getting to a little bit of more...

00:09:50.650 --> 00:09:52.480
One of the specific areas of interest

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:55.480
that actually I personally
have, I work on vaccines

00:09:55.480 --> 00:09:58.873
as part of my sort of
a major focus of work,

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:02.360
a lot of these situations result

00:10:02.360 --> 00:10:05.270
in disruption of immunization efforts

00:10:05.270 --> 00:10:10.270
and as I understand IRC
has worked with partners

00:10:11.750 --> 00:10:16.580
to sort of bridge that gap
in some of these populations.

00:10:16.580 --> 00:10:18.640
So what is the state
of vaccination efforts

00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:21.908
in countries where the IRC operates

00:10:21.908 --> 00:10:24.880
what are some of the key challenges

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:27.830
and what would it take to bridge that gap?

00:10:27.830 --> 00:10:29.560
- Yeah, well, look there are two parts

00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:30.420
of the answer to that question,

00:10:30.420 --> 00:10:33.030
I think one is as if we were
having this conversation

00:10:33.030 --> 00:10:34.120
two years ago

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:37.140
and then there's the answer
in the wake of COVID,

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:39.123
so let me address separately.

00:10:40.210 --> 00:10:42.190
Most of the places we work

00:10:42.190 --> 00:10:45.140
either have no effective health system

00:10:45.140 --> 00:10:48.110
or a very weak health system

00:10:48.110 --> 00:10:49.700
sometimes marked by conflict

00:10:49.700 --> 00:10:52.617
between the governing
authorities of the places we work

00:10:52.617 --> 00:10:54.540
and the national authorities.

00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:56.170
So in the democratic Republic of Congo

00:10:56.170 --> 00:10:57.770
during the Ebola crisis,

00:10:57.770 --> 00:11:00.850
health centers were being attacked

00:11:01.966 --> 00:11:05.920
because they were perceived
to be an imposition

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:06.850
of the central government

00:11:06.850 --> 00:11:10.930
and the international
agencies were seen as allies

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:11.840
of the central government

00:11:11.840 --> 00:11:15.340
in opposition to local
or political opposition

00:11:15.340 --> 00:11:19.000
in the in Eastern Kivu and or
in the east of the country.

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:21.640
And this were raises a
very interesting point,

00:11:21.640 --> 00:11:23.590
there was really quite
spectacular progress

00:11:23.590 --> 00:11:25.670
towards the Millennium Development Goals

00:11:25.670 --> 00:11:27.600
that were enunciated in 2000

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:30.420
people talked in 2015
about being halfway there.

00:11:30.420 --> 00:11:32.690
And the problem is the
second half of the match

00:11:32.690 --> 00:11:35.890
to provide global health coverage

00:11:35.890 --> 00:11:39.140
is completely different than
the first half of the match,

00:11:39.140 --> 00:11:40.300
because the first for the match

00:11:40.300 --> 00:11:41.840
was about health system strengthening,

00:11:41.840 --> 00:11:43.150
whereas the second half of the match

00:11:43.150 --> 00:11:45.240
is well, what you do when

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:48.130
really it's a much more
conflicted situation?

00:11:48.130 --> 00:11:50.530
And it's very hard to
think about a system,

00:11:50.530 --> 00:11:51.560
there isn't yet a system

00:11:51.560 --> 00:11:54.421
and that's where we have been working

00:11:54.421 --> 00:11:56.800
and where we've tried
to break out of the idea

00:11:56.800 --> 00:12:00.060
that we are just the "emergency" sector

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:03.450
that we are there when
people fall down off a cliff,

00:12:03.450 --> 00:12:04.380
people use this cliche,

00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:06.330
we should build fences
at the top of cliffs,

00:12:06.330 --> 00:12:08.212
not just to help people
at the bottom of cliffs,

00:12:08.212 --> 00:12:11.310
that's wrong increasingly
for the places we work,

00:12:11.310 --> 00:12:12.770
because there are chronic problems

00:12:12.770 --> 00:12:15.810
not just disaster of
someone falling off a cliff.

00:12:15.810 --> 00:12:20.810
Now, the COVID crisis has both
fed off those inequalities

00:12:21.302 --> 00:12:23.190
and exacerbated those inequalities,

00:12:23.190 --> 00:12:25.490
I mean, the basic answer to your question,

00:12:25.490 --> 00:12:27.960
what's the vaccine situation
in the places that you work?

00:12:27.960 --> 00:12:29.390
I mean, there basically
aren't any vaccines,

00:12:29.390 --> 00:12:31.810
I mean, that's more or less the situation,

00:12:31.810 --> 00:12:35.460
I mean, it's the most stupid
piece of short termism

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:36.760
on the part of the global community,

00:12:36.760 --> 00:12:38.270
not to have global vaccination,

00:12:38.270 --> 00:12:40.570
I don't need to have the rhetoric on that,

00:12:40.570 --> 00:12:43.520
I'm sure you've got the
facts to back it up,

00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:45.930
but essentially it's the fog of war

00:12:45.930 --> 00:12:47.537
when it comes to fighting COVID
in the places that we work

00:12:47.537 --> 00:12:49.520
'cause there isn't proper testing,

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:51.510
so no one really knows who's got it.

00:12:51.510 --> 00:12:55.052
And there isn't proper vaccination

00:12:55.052 --> 00:12:56.820
and in the first year of the crisis,

00:12:56.820 --> 00:13:01.750
there was very weak
public health measures.

00:13:01.750 --> 00:13:04.220
I mean, there are information campaigns,

00:13:04.220 --> 00:13:05.620
but there's a lot of disinformation,

00:13:05.620 --> 00:13:07.770
there are attempts to
promote hand washing,

00:13:07.770 --> 00:13:12.770
there isn't enough water you
know the story quite well.

00:13:13.360 --> 00:13:16.980
There's probably a overmedicalization
of the idea of health

00:13:16.980 --> 00:13:19.754
when, of course pandemics require

00:13:19.754 --> 00:13:21.950
thought and action
beyond the health sector.

00:13:21.950 --> 00:13:23.644
I'm not sure if I'm allowed to say that

00:13:23.644 --> 00:13:27.270
in the Yale Institute
of Global Public Health,

00:13:27.270 --> 00:13:30.830
but that's the two answer

00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:33.748
that I would give to your question.

00:13:33.748 --> 00:13:35.350
- No, no, you're absolutely right

00:13:35.350 --> 00:13:37.420
and one of the things
that we do here at YIGH

00:13:37.420 --> 00:13:38.750
at the Yale Institute for Global Health

00:13:38.750 --> 00:13:42.253
is go beyond the traditional
biomedical assets

00:13:42.253 --> 00:13:45.170
that you bring that you
bring to these problems

00:13:45.170 --> 00:13:49.610
because we know that if
global health solutions

00:13:49.610 --> 00:13:53.130
are only draw from a
limited number of fields,

00:13:53.130 --> 00:13:55.807
you always almost invariably fall short

00:13:55.807 --> 00:13:58.680
and it's not just true for pandemics

00:13:58.680 --> 00:14:03.610
or sort of displaced
populations, et cetera.

00:14:03.610 --> 00:14:07.160
It's true for HIV and
noncommunicable diseases

00:14:07.160 --> 00:14:09.460
and all of that stuff, but
you're absolutely right.

00:14:09.460 --> 00:14:11.810
So you alluded to that and
you have previously described

00:14:11.810 --> 00:14:15.342
and you've used the term,
"the age of impunity"

00:14:15.342 --> 00:14:18.030
specifically in the context of attacks

00:14:18.030 --> 00:14:20.270
on healthcare workers, et cetera.

00:14:20.270 --> 00:14:22.960
And there was like sort
of you miss something

00:14:22.960 --> 00:14:24.693
when you don't have it,

00:14:26.647 --> 00:14:29.713
there used to be maybe obviously,

00:14:29.713 --> 00:14:34.010
the past looks rosier than it's often is,

00:14:34.010 --> 00:14:37.240
but doesn't it seem and I
think you were alluding to that

00:14:37.240 --> 00:14:42.240
that it's more of direct
attacks on healthcare workers,

00:14:43.110 --> 00:14:45.990
at least that perception is there

00:14:45.990 --> 00:14:50.990
and so how do we get back to
at least what was there before

00:14:51.220 --> 00:14:56.220
or at least a better normal
in that kind of a situation,

00:14:56.340 --> 00:14:58.910
and you are uniquely positioned

00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:02.991
because of your political
background in there.

00:15:02.991 --> 00:15:05.210
And so how do we get back to that

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:08.620
sort of unsaid packed
between various players?

00:15:08.620 --> 00:15:10.840
- Well, I think first of all,

00:15:10.840 --> 00:15:13.160
we recognize that the
fact that it was unsaid

00:15:13.160 --> 00:15:14.365
was a weakness and not a strength.

00:15:14.365 --> 00:15:15.198
- Yeah.

00:15:15.198 --> 00:15:18.820
- And essentially the bargain post 1945,

00:15:18.820 --> 00:15:22.600
was that in parallel or in
addition to the rights of states,

00:15:22.600 --> 00:15:25.852
there'd be rights of individuals,
universal human rights

00:15:25.852 --> 00:15:28.240
and when I talk about the age of impunity,

00:15:28.240 --> 00:15:31.580
what I refer to is the
abrogation of those rights.

00:15:31.580 --> 00:15:32.520
Impunity as you know,

00:15:32.520 --> 00:15:34.260
is actions without consequence,

00:15:34.260 --> 00:15:35.950
crimes without punishment

00:15:35.950 --> 00:15:38.390
and when it comes to
attacks on health workers,

00:15:38.390 --> 00:15:41.180
you've got a classic case
of rights to livelihood

00:15:41.180 --> 00:15:43.370
and civilian protection in conflict,

00:15:43.370 --> 00:15:45.750
but then being abrogated
by conflict players.

00:15:45.750 --> 00:15:48.504
I mean, Syria is the most infamous example

00:15:48.504 --> 00:15:51.370
where attacks on health facilities

00:15:51.370 --> 00:15:54.774
actually increased after a UN
Security Council Resolution

00:15:54.774 --> 00:15:58.020
that condemned attacks on health workers

00:15:58.020 --> 00:15:59.510
and called for them to cease.

00:15:59.510 --> 00:16:03.170
So, you can see the impunity
in the thumbing of the nose,

00:16:03.170 --> 00:16:06.560
again, in the face of the UN
Security Council Resolution.

00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:08.870
Obviously, this requires us to think

00:16:08.870 --> 00:16:11.100
not just about a humanitarian
answer, but a political answer

00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:14.810
because every humanitarian
crisis is a political crisis

00:16:14.810 --> 00:16:17.770
to every humanitarian emergency
is a political emergency.

00:16:17.770 --> 00:16:22.413
And in the launch of our
emergency watch list this year,

00:16:23.340 --> 00:16:24.810
I talked about the humanitarian sector,

00:16:24.810 --> 00:16:26.870
but I also ventured into geopolitics

00:16:26.870 --> 00:16:29.550
to say that we had a
responsibility to bear witness

00:16:29.550 --> 00:16:30.690
to what was happening

00:16:30.690 --> 00:16:34.980
and call out the failure
of the international system

00:16:34.980 --> 00:16:36.740
to uphold its own norms

00:16:36.740 --> 00:16:38.930
and then to suggest ways
that they could be upheld.

00:16:38.930 --> 00:16:40.640
Let me just give you one interesting,

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:42.710
I think, example of this
in the interest of not

00:16:42.710 --> 00:16:44.770
and I could speak for the
next 40 minutes about this,

00:16:44.770 --> 00:16:45.920
so I won't.

00:16:45.920 --> 00:16:50.920
So at the moment, the veto
in the UN Security Council,

00:16:51.480 --> 00:16:53.766
which is held by five countries,

00:16:53.766 --> 00:16:57.980
US obviously, UK, France,
China, and Russia,

00:16:57.980 --> 00:16:59.990
the only veto wielding countries

00:16:59.990 --> 00:17:02.193
reflecting the balance
of power after 1945,

00:17:03.980 --> 00:17:06.140
there is a French proposal

00:17:06.140 --> 00:17:11.140
that the veto should be suspended
in cases of mass atrocity.

00:17:11.700 --> 00:17:15.300
Now, that is one way of
shifting the dice a bit

00:17:15.300 --> 00:17:16.470
in favor of the civilians

00:17:16.470 --> 00:17:19.840
who are meant to be having
protections under the UN Charter.

00:17:19.840 --> 00:17:21.580
That's not an answer and
it probably won't happen,

00:17:21.580 --> 00:17:23.319
but it's the kind of thing

00:17:23.319 --> 00:17:26.277
that would actually shift
the balance of power.

00:17:26.277 --> 00:17:29.860
'Cause at the moment to
state the obvious, I suppose,

00:17:29.860 --> 00:17:32.630
impunity reflects imbalance of power

00:17:32.630 --> 00:17:35.210
and you have to have a
countervailing power,

00:17:35.210 --> 00:17:37.240
Galbraith it called a countervailing power

00:17:37.240 --> 00:17:38.690
to push against that.

00:17:38.690 --> 00:17:41.230
Now, to the extent that shame doesn't work

00:17:41.230 --> 00:17:42.400
as countervailing power,

00:17:42.400 --> 00:17:44.330
to the extent that economic sanction

00:17:44.330 --> 00:17:46.195
doesn't work as countervailing power,

00:17:46.195 --> 00:17:48.280
to the extent that
arming opposition groups,

00:17:48.280 --> 00:17:49.210
which some countries do

00:17:49.210 --> 00:17:51.810
doesn't work as countervailing power,

00:17:51.810 --> 00:17:53.850
you need to get to the root
of the imbalance of power,

00:17:53.850 --> 00:17:55.920
which is essentially the conflict actors,

00:17:55.920 --> 00:17:57.220
think they've got protection,

00:17:57.220 --> 00:18:01.100
as a protection racket for
their attacks on health workers.

00:18:01.100 --> 00:18:03.500
And that's what I think
one has to understand

00:18:03.500 --> 00:18:06.830
is the root of this problem
and it needs to be called out.

00:18:06.830 --> 00:18:11.250
And obviously the Syrian example

00:18:11.250 --> 00:18:14.320
allows those of us in the Western world

00:18:14.320 --> 00:18:16.820
to blame the Russians and the Iranians

00:18:16.820 --> 00:18:18.730
and the Syrian government.

00:18:18.730 --> 00:18:19.800
But the Yemen example,

00:18:19.800 --> 00:18:21.810
I'm afraid it plays the other way

00:18:21.810 --> 00:18:26.279
where Saudi led coalition, which
includes the US and the UK,

00:18:26.279 --> 00:18:28.720
hasn't been targeting health
workers in the same way,

00:18:28.720 --> 00:18:30.840
but as you probably
know 43 kids got killed

00:18:30.840 --> 00:18:33.143
in a coach load in Northern Yemen,

00:18:34.160 --> 00:18:38.320
which is also as close to a
war crime as you can imagine.

00:18:38.320 --> 00:18:42.780
So, those in glass houses
have got to be careful

00:18:42.780 --> 00:18:45.050
about throwing stones and there are...

00:18:45.050 --> 00:18:49.010
I don't want to draw
equivalence between if you like

00:18:49.010 --> 00:18:51.560
the authoritarian autocratic
parts of the world

00:18:51.560 --> 00:18:52.393
and the democratic parts of the world,

00:18:52.393 --> 00:18:54.820
'cause again that's a grievous mistake,

00:18:54.820 --> 00:18:59.820
but I don't want to seem
blind to the failings

00:18:59.960 --> 00:19:01.690
or to the mistakes that have been made

00:19:01.690 --> 00:19:03.593
on "our side" as well.

00:19:05.070 --> 00:19:05.903
- Oh, that's interesting

00:19:05.903 --> 00:19:10.903
and the fact that you
mentioned this a political

00:19:11.281 --> 00:19:14.280
sort of initiative to address this.

00:19:14.280 --> 00:19:18.480
If I can say so in our
institutions there...

00:19:19.654 --> 00:19:24.654
My colleagues can be fairly
loud for the right course,

00:19:28.280 --> 00:19:30.432
so I'm sure you'll find allies

00:19:30.432 --> 00:19:31.530
and I know that-
- But I would love you

00:19:31.530 --> 00:19:32.370
to be loud.

00:19:32.370 --> 00:19:35.950
I mean, there's essentially
impunity thrives on darkness

00:19:35.950 --> 00:19:39.450
and on quietude and essentially

00:19:39.450 --> 00:19:43.338
the 70% of victims in war today

00:19:43.338 --> 00:19:46.293
are of are civilians not soldiers.

00:19:47.300 --> 00:19:50.582
- Yeah, absolutely and so
if you need more voices

00:19:50.582 --> 00:19:53.630
and voice is something
that needs to be amplified

00:19:54.677 --> 00:19:57.120
by sort of actors and advocates,

00:19:57.120 --> 00:19:58.930
especially health workers,
but not exclusively.

00:19:58.930 --> 00:20:00.830
So feel free to send it our way

00:20:00.830 --> 00:20:02.922
and you would be happy to as an aside,

00:20:02.922 --> 00:20:04.950
circulate that.
- Thank you.

00:20:04.950 --> 00:20:08.230
And so another aspect that has...

00:20:09.280 --> 00:20:11.070
I'm not exaggerating if I would say

00:20:11.070 --> 00:20:13.590
that it's a near existential threat

00:20:13.590 --> 00:20:16.730
or if not an existential
threat, is the climate crisis

00:20:16.730 --> 00:20:18.064
and actually for vulnerable communities,

00:20:18.064 --> 00:20:20.020
it is an existential threat.

00:20:20.020 --> 00:20:23.425
So in your work, how have
you seen climate change

00:20:23.425 --> 00:20:27.870
affect how conflicts occur and unfold

00:20:27.870 --> 00:20:30.380
and not just the number of conflicts,

00:20:30.380 --> 00:20:34.223
but the nature of conflicts
and specially going forward?

00:20:35.549 --> 00:20:38.990
- Yeah, I mean, this is an area
where it's easy to be glib,

00:20:38.990 --> 00:20:40.140
but also easy to be wrong.

00:20:40.140 --> 00:20:42.900
I mean, a lot of people
talk about climate refugees

00:20:42.900 --> 00:20:45.950
and that doesn't yet fit the facts.

00:20:45.950 --> 00:20:47.960
I mean, we've got a lot of climate IDPs

00:20:47.960 --> 00:20:49.250
internally displaced people,

00:20:49.250 --> 00:20:51.870
but not that many people who
cross international borders

00:20:51.870 --> 00:20:53.450
as a result of climate crisis,

00:20:53.450 --> 00:20:55.000
although you can imagine that happening.

00:20:55.000 --> 00:20:57.227
You asked about climate and conflict

00:20:57.227 --> 00:21:00.540
and I see this in a couple of ways.

00:21:00.540 --> 00:21:03.000
One is that obviously the climate crisis

00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:04.340
contributes to resource stress

00:21:04.340 --> 00:21:06.670
and resource stress is one
of the drivers of conflict,

00:21:06.670 --> 00:21:10.760
so you've got an indirect impact there.

00:21:10.760 --> 00:21:13.540
Secondly, there's no question
that some of the people

00:21:13.540 --> 00:21:15.820
that we serve notably in
the Sahel, but not only

00:21:15.820 --> 00:21:20.570
you'll know well that
there is Syria Scholarship

00:21:20.570 --> 00:21:24.469
about how the climate
crisis in Northwestern Syria

00:21:24.469 --> 00:21:27.060
was a contributor to the
flow of people to the cities

00:21:27.060 --> 00:21:31.173
in 2008, 2011 and that contributed
to the Syrian uprising,

00:21:32.050 --> 00:21:34.150
I don't want to opine on that here.

00:21:34.150 --> 00:21:37.870
But there's no question that a good number

00:21:37.870 --> 00:21:40.109
of the communities that we serve

00:21:40.109 --> 00:21:42.880
have done very little to
contribute to the climate crisis,

00:21:42.880 --> 00:21:47.073
but are on the receiving end
of its pernicious effects

00:21:47.073 --> 00:21:50.950
and they move or they are impoverished.

00:21:50.950 --> 00:21:53.590
And one of the things that we
are doing now is building...

00:21:53.590 --> 00:21:57.410
We rewrote our strategy
a year and a half ago

00:21:57.410 --> 00:21:59.890
and we now talk about the climate crisis

00:21:59.890 --> 00:22:04.890
clearly as a driver of humanitarian need.

00:22:05.020 --> 00:22:07.040
In our watch list of a year ago,

00:22:07.040 --> 00:22:08.718
not the most recent, but the one before,

00:22:08.718 --> 00:22:12.240
we talked about conflict climate and COVID

00:22:12.240 --> 00:22:13.930
as the drivers of humanitarian need

00:22:13.930 --> 00:22:17.540
and that obviously remains
significantly the case.

00:22:17.540 --> 00:22:18.930
There's more scholarship to be done

00:22:18.930 --> 00:22:21.280
about exactly how that follows through

00:22:21.280 --> 00:22:23.550
into migratory patterns,

00:22:23.550 --> 00:22:25.770
but it's obvious that
it's going to be real,

00:22:25.770 --> 00:22:26.840
although to emphasize,

00:22:26.840 --> 00:22:28.960
I think it's more likely
to be an IDP issue,

00:22:28.960 --> 00:22:30.300
an internal displacement issue

00:22:30.300 --> 00:22:32.824
of which there are about just
so your audience may know

00:22:32.824 --> 00:22:34.890
there's about 35 million refugees

00:22:34.890 --> 00:22:36.060
and asylum seekers around the world,

00:22:36.060 --> 00:22:38.300
those are people who've
left their own country

00:22:38.300 --> 00:22:41.150
as a result of effectively politics,

00:22:41.150 --> 00:22:42.720
as opposed to economics.

00:22:42.720 --> 00:22:45.380
But there's 45 million people
who are internally displaced

00:22:45.380 --> 00:22:48.393
as a result of conflict
and related issues,

00:22:49.400 --> 00:22:51.920
Syria being a prime example
both of refugee flows

00:22:51.920 --> 00:22:54.130
and of IDP flows.

00:22:54.130 --> 00:22:58.670
And obviously we see, we fear,

00:22:58.670 --> 00:23:01.180
we anticipate the climate crisis

00:23:01.180 --> 00:23:04.563
as a conflict multiplier, if you like.

00:23:06.710 --> 00:23:10.987
- Yeah, so in terms of the kinds of things

00:23:15.230 --> 00:23:17.040
communities can do around the world,

00:23:17.040 --> 00:23:19.560
like our community here in New Haven,

00:23:19.560 --> 00:23:24.560
anything specific that
communities like New Haven can do

00:23:27.970 --> 00:23:29.640
our host town for this university

00:23:29.640 --> 00:23:31.290
to support refugee communities,

00:23:31.290 --> 00:23:36.290
we are again, a center where
a lot of refugee settlement

00:23:37.280 --> 00:23:40.790
is happening, including
in the recent crisis,

00:23:40.790 --> 00:23:44.122
but what are some of the things
that individual communities

00:23:44.122 --> 00:23:49.110
can do to help out with these crises?

00:23:49.110 --> 00:23:51.010
- Well, the IRC is a good organization

00:23:51.010 --> 00:23:51.860
to ask that question to,

00:23:51.860 --> 00:23:54.570
because we work across the arc of crisis,

00:23:54.570 --> 00:23:56.539
we're an international
humanitarian aid agency,

00:23:56.539 --> 00:23:59.070
but we're also a refugee
resettlement agency

00:23:59.070 --> 00:24:00.850
in 25 US cities,

00:24:00.850 --> 00:24:04.300
we're the largest refugee
resettlement agency in America

00:24:04.300 --> 00:24:06.810
and I like to point out we're
actually the number one rated,

00:24:06.810 --> 00:24:08.800
which is the more important
than being the largest.

00:24:08.800 --> 00:24:13.800
Now, sadly, we're not allocated
the office in New Haven

00:24:15.100 --> 00:24:17.190
and I'm not sure whether
it's Catholic Charities

00:24:17.190 --> 00:24:19.860
or the Lutheran World Service

00:24:19.860 --> 00:24:21.803
or HIAS, the Hebrew Immigrant Aid Society

00:24:21.803 --> 00:24:26.600
that is the lead agency in New Haven.

00:24:26.600 --> 00:24:28.540
But President Biden has said

00:24:28.540 --> 00:24:31.520
he wants to increase
from 15,000 to 125,000,

00:24:31.520 --> 00:24:34.670
the number of refugees let
into America every year,

00:24:34.670 --> 00:24:36.393
this was before the Afghan crisis.

00:24:38.950 --> 00:24:39.783
People don't know this,

00:24:39.783 --> 00:24:41.290
but Ronald Reagan let him more refugees

00:24:41.290 --> 00:24:42.530
than any other American president

00:24:42.530 --> 00:24:44.820
after the 1980 Refugee Act

00:24:44.820 --> 00:24:47.090
was passed under President Carter.

00:24:47.090 --> 00:24:50.276
And I'm afraid President
Trump ran the program

00:24:50.276 --> 00:24:53.350
almost into the ground
down to 12,000 refugees.

00:24:53.350 --> 00:24:55.690
President Biden said he
would increase it to 125,000,

00:24:55.690 --> 00:24:57.380
it'll take a year or two to do that.

00:24:57.380 --> 00:24:59.870
But then we've got the
70,000 Afghans who arrived

00:24:59.870 --> 00:25:03.280
and there will be Afghans
arriving in New Haven,

00:25:03.280 --> 00:25:06.150
just like there will be
Congolese or South Sudanese

00:25:06.150 --> 00:25:08.680
or Rohingya from Myanmar.

00:25:08.680 --> 00:25:10.490
And what those people need is the services

00:25:10.490 --> 00:25:13.170
of resettlement agencies,
but they also need buddies,

00:25:13.170 --> 00:25:14.510
they also need English teachers,

00:25:14.510 --> 00:25:16.770
they also need people to explain to them

00:25:16.770 --> 00:25:19.710
how the American healthcare system works.

00:25:19.710 --> 00:25:24.110
And I can say very strongly to you,

00:25:24.110 --> 00:25:26.850
even though we don't work in New Haven,

00:25:26.850 --> 00:25:28.320
it's not one of the places
that we are allocated

00:25:28.320 --> 00:25:29.370
by the state department,

00:25:29.370 --> 00:25:31.710
there's a lot for you to do there.

00:25:31.710 --> 00:25:32.600
I'm actually in London at the moment,

00:25:32.600 --> 00:25:34.060
that's why I've got this
beautiful background

00:25:34.060 --> 00:25:36.710
that you can see from this
rather Spartan Hotel room.

00:25:37.560 --> 00:25:39.900
But I was meeting some refugees

00:25:39.900 --> 00:25:42.190
that we are resettling here
in the Southeast of England

00:25:42.190 --> 00:25:43.917
this morning from
Afghanistan and from Syria

00:25:43.917 --> 00:25:46.170
and they need cultural orientation

00:25:46.170 --> 00:25:48.000
when you've gone through
the trauma of displacement

00:25:48.000 --> 00:25:49.830
is a massive thing.

00:25:49.830 --> 00:25:51.887
And so you can do good locally,

00:25:51.887 --> 00:25:54.989
as well as using the
extraordinary platform of Yale

00:25:54.989 --> 00:25:56.600
to reach out globally.

00:25:56.600 --> 00:25:57.880
I mean, reaching out globally,

00:25:57.880 --> 00:26:01.350
we are the largest
impact evaluation agency

00:26:01.350 --> 00:26:03.760
in the humanitarian sector
and we do work with you,

00:26:03.760 --> 00:26:06.030
we could probably do more about what works

00:26:06.030 --> 00:26:07.420
in humanitarian circumstances,

00:26:07.420 --> 00:26:08.253
we have something called

00:26:08.253 --> 00:26:09.490
the Outcomes and Evidence Framework,

00:26:09.490 --> 00:26:10.640
that you can see online,

00:26:10.640 --> 00:26:12.690
which is what our country directors see.

00:26:12.690 --> 00:26:14.270
So you can do more

00:26:14.270 --> 00:26:16.930
through your professional
responsibilities.

00:26:16.930 --> 00:26:18.550
And then as you were saying earlier,

00:26:18.550 --> 00:26:21.539
there's your voice both
individually and collectively

00:26:21.539 --> 00:26:24.840
because the COVID crisis has shown

00:26:24.840 --> 00:26:29.270
that even when disaster
is staring global leaders

00:26:29.270 --> 00:26:32.780
in the face, they can
avoid the responsibility

00:26:32.780 --> 00:26:34.890
of tending the global comments

00:26:34.890 --> 00:26:37.060
and that I think should
be extremely worrying

00:26:37.060 --> 00:26:38.310
whether you're in the humanitarian sector

00:26:38.310 --> 00:26:40.180
or in the health sector.

00:26:40.180 --> 00:26:42.810
- Absolutely, so switching
gear gears a little bit,

00:26:42.810 --> 00:26:46.370
but you played a role
in the Independent Panel

00:26:46.370 --> 00:26:48.383
for Pandemic Preparedness and Response,

00:26:51.470 --> 00:26:54.530
it was perfectly involved
through my next door neighbor

00:26:54.530 --> 00:26:56.763
Ernesto Zedillo-
- Oh, wow.

00:26:57.715 --> 00:27:00.180
- Who's a faculty member
here at Yale as well

00:27:00.180 --> 00:27:03.330
and the panel prepared a very interesting

00:27:03.330 --> 00:27:06.218
and insightful report,

00:27:06.218 --> 00:27:09.623
but this was around the time

00:27:09.623 --> 00:27:11.683
that the report came out in early 2021,

00:27:17.571 --> 00:27:22.571
this was pre-Delta, it was
around Alpha and now Omicron

00:27:23.900 --> 00:27:27.980
and it was around the
time when we had the hope

00:27:27.980 --> 00:27:30.270
and somewhat some would say
that the naive illusion,

00:27:30.270 --> 00:27:31.860
that low income countries

00:27:31.860 --> 00:27:34.230
will get the vaccines fairly quickly.

00:27:34.230 --> 00:27:36.130
So things have changed since then

00:27:37.707 --> 00:27:39.740
and so any thoughts about,

00:27:39.740 --> 00:27:43.160
like in addition to bearing witness

00:27:43.160 --> 00:27:44.823
to this enormous strategy,

00:27:45.850 --> 00:27:48.037
what are the prospects of
some of the recommendations

00:27:48.037 --> 00:27:50.200
and feel free to highlight
any specific recommendations-

00:27:50.200 --> 00:27:54.120
- So look, let me talk about the panel

00:27:54.120 --> 00:27:57.220
and what it said and to
what it's been ignored

00:27:57.220 --> 00:27:59.770
and then let me talk
also on the same page,

00:27:59.770 --> 00:28:02.150
the International Panel on
Pandemic Preparedness Response

00:28:02.150 --> 00:28:05.890
was set up by the World Health Assembly,

00:28:05.890 --> 00:28:06.723
it was independent,

00:28:06.723 --> 00:28:08.625
it was chaired by two
former prime ministers,

00:28:08.625 --> 00:28:11.810
Helen Clark and Ellen Johnson Sirleaf.

00:28:11.810 --> 00:28:15.300
It had about 15 people who
included experts in health,

00:28:15.300 --> 00:28:17.200
but was not dominated by health experts

00:28:17.200 --> 00:28:19.100
and the fundamental
insight was that pandemics

00:28:19.100 --> 00:28:21.750
are not a health issue, they
are a health and security

00:28:21.750 --> 00:28:24.690
and politics and economic issue.

00:28:24.690 --> 00:28:29.500
And the recommendations
spoke to that central point

00:28:29.500 --> 00:28:33.210
that pandemic control
required political leadership

00:28:33.210 --> 00:28:34.043
at the highest level,

00:28:34.043 --> 00:28:36.460
it needed additional
funding through health

00:28:36.460 --> 00:28:38.450
but also non-health institutions,

00:28:38.450 --> 00:28:40.313
it required a commitment to equity.

00:28:41.170 --> 00:28:44.123
And there was a set of
structural recommendations

00:28:44.123 --> 00:28:48.700
for the World Health
Organization, but also beyond it,

00:28:48.700 --> 00:28:51.110
because our argument was
that if everything gets left

00:28:51.110 --> 00:28:53.048
to the WHO, it won't get done,

00:28:53.048 --> 00:28:54.700
because it goes beyond health.

00:28:54.700 --> 00:28:56.460
Now, what we got wrong I think,

00:28:56.460 --> 00:29:01.090
or what was a tactical error,
is our recommendations,

00:29:01.090 --> 00:29:05.030
whether about PPE or production facilities

00:29:05.030 --> 00:29:08.680
or intellectual property
were about future pandemic.

00:29:08.680 --> 00:29:11.470
What we should have recognized
is that what we need

00:29:11.470 --> 00:29:13.400
in terms of preparing ourselves

00:29:13.400 --> 00:29:14.830
and responding to future pandemics,

00:29:14.830 --> 00:29:17.360
we actually need now for
the current pandemic.

00:29:17.360 --> 00:29:19.700
And we had recommendations
for the current pandemic,

00:29:19.700 --> 00:29:21.790
but they were separate
from the recommendations

00:29:21.790 --> 00:29:23.200
for the future.

00:29:23.200 --> 00:29:27.110
What strikes me is that, we
were published in May, 2021,

00:29:27.110 --> 00:29:31.260
so the it's not quite a year,

00:29:31.260 --> 00:29:35.440
but the health machinery through
the World Health Assembly

00:29:35.440 --> 00:29:37.433
and the negotiating machinery there,

00:29:39.070 --> 00:29:42.920
doesn't have the political
sway to deliver the money

00:29:42.920 --> 00:29:46.770
and the political accountability

00:29:46.770 --> 00:29:49.490
that pandemic preparedness
and response requires

00:29:49.490 --> 00:29:52.690
and so we've got a
mismatch between the places

00:29:52.690 --> 00:29:56.040
that are negotiating
internationally on this

00:29:56.040 --> 00:29:57.780
and the places that have real power.

00:29:57.780 --> 00:30:00.481
Now, President Biden and
his team to be fair to them,

00:30:00.481 --> 00:30:02.820
they've held One Leader Level Summit,

00:30:02.820 --> 00:30:04.290
they're having another one in March

00:30:04.290 --> 00:30:07.210
to try to break out of this conundrum

00:30:07.210 --> 00:30:10.520
that the health infrastructure
for negotiation takes years

00:30:10.520 --> 00:30:13.060
and we haven't got yours on our side.

00:30:13.060 --> 00:30:16.530
But you'll know that the
US is also quite conflicted

00:30:16.530 --> 00:30:21.530
about the WHO and the
WHO is two-third funded

00:30:22.400 --> 00:30:23.500
by voluntary contributions.

00:30:23.500 --> 00:30:27.377
That's not a basis for a
sustainable independence of mind

00:30:29.783 --> 00:30:32.010
and we argued very strongly that the WHO

00:30:32.010 --> 00:30:33.570
needed to be more independent

00:30:33.570 --> 00:30:36.360
and more powerful to hold
countries to account.

00:30:36.360 --> 00:30:39.590
And I think it's a real
struggle at the moment,

00:30:39.590 --> 00:30:41.870
hopefully the next summit
that President Biden holds

00:30:41.870 --> 00:30:44.217
will help address the current crisis

00:30:44.217 --> 00:30:46.820
and in the process help
prepare us for the next one,

00:30:46.820 --> 00:30:48.370
because you'll know this better than I,

00:30:48.370 --> 00:30:52.378
but my reading suggests
we got lucky with Omicron

00:30:52.378 --> 00:30:54.880
and it could easily have
been more transmissible

00:30:54.880 --> 00:30:56.530
than Delta but also more virulent

00:30:57.786 --> 00:31:00.483
and we've got off in some sense slightly.

00:31:01.910 --> 00:31:05.854
- Yeah, and there's no
guarantee that a variant

00:31:05.854 --> 00:31:09.378
that is more transmissible
will have lower virulence

00:31:09.378 --> 00:31:11.440
that seems to be a misconception

00:31:11.440 --> 00:31:13.160
that there's some sort of magic trade off

00:31:13.160 --> 00:31:16.320
that the virus makes with itself...

00:31:17.240 --> 00:31:19.110
And sort of evolutionary
biology has told us

00:31:19.110 --> 00:31:22.168
that no, that doesn't have to be the case

00:31:22.168 --> 00:31:24.840
in these kinds of situations.

00:31:24.840 --> 00:31:29.670
So, I'm now taking one
question from the audience

00:31:29.670 --> 00:31:32.350
and the first question from there is,

00:31:32.350 --> 00:31:35.002
how do you think today's change
in the liberal world order

00:31:35.002 --> 00:31:38.560
as compared to when it
was first established

00:31:38.560 --> 00:31:39.913
and you can add nuance,

00:31:40.830 --> 00:31:43.760
when was the prime of
the liberal world order?

00:31:43.760 --> 00:31:46.720
It never a sort of a
perfect moment for that,

00:31:46.720 --> 00:31:49.460
but let's suppose that
the current situation

00:31:49.460 --> 00:31:52.757
has seen changes and emergence of players

00:31:54.394 --> 00:31:56.739
that are decidedly illiberal

00:31:56.739 --> 00:32:00.080
in the sort of nonpartisan
sense of the world.

00:32:00.080 --> 00:32:04.470
And how would that change
humanitarian efforts

00:32:04.470 --> 00:32:05.617
in public health?

00:32:07.580 --> 00:32:08.857
- Well, I think first of all,

00:32:08.857 --> 00:32:10.640
it's really important that we don't fall

00:32:10.640 --> 00:32:11.970
into a sort of golden ageism,

00:32:11.970 --> 00:32:16.970
the cold war was not a golden
age for obvious reasons.

00:32:18.100 --> 00:32:20.440
Secondly, I do think
without wishing to fall

00:32:20.440 --> 00:32:22.550
into being panglosian about it,

00:32:22.550 --> 00:32:24.410
there was a period in
the early two thousands,

00:32:24.410 --> 00:32:26.370
it was a brief shining moment

00:32:26.370 --> 00:32:30.640
when ideas of global
responsibility really took root.

00:32:30.640 --> 00:32:33.300
It wasn't a product just
of the end of the cold war,

00:32:33.300 --> 00:32:35.910
it was a product of the failings in Rwanda

00:32:35.910 --> 00:32:39.326
and in former Yugoslavia
and that led to this period

00:32:39.326 --> 00:32:42.260
where there was a real
discussion up until 2006

00:32:42.260 --> 00:32:44.580
about international responsibilities

00:32:44.580 --> 00:32:47.010
and the extent to which state sovereignty

00:32:47.010 --> 00:32:50.934
was an insufficient
basis on which to manage

00:32:50.934 --> 00:32:53.410
an interdependent world order.

00:32:53.410 --> 00:32:55.520
And I prefer to talk
about a rules-based order

00:32:55.520 --> 00:32:57.008
rather than a liberal international order.

00:32:57.008 --> 00:32:57.841
- Yeah.

00:32:57.841 --> 00:33:02.841
- 'Cause I think that
liberal is too complicated

00:33:02.979 --> 00:33:06.523
for too many people, I
mean, just as a digression,

00:33:07.820 --> 00:33:10.040
that if you remember what
I said 20 minutes ago,

00:33:10.040 --> 00:33:11.440
about rights for individuals,

00:33:11.440 --> 00:33:13.320
that is the essence of a liberal ideal

00:33:13.320 --> 00:33:15.700
that individuals have have rights.

00:33:15.700 --> 00:33:17.410
But sometimes when we talk about

00:33:17.410 --> 00:33:18.520
liberal international order,

00:33:18.520 --> 00:33:20.510
we lose side the fact
that it was about rules

00:33:20.510 --> 00:33:22.403
that would govern the powerful

00:33:22.403 --> 00:33:25.200
and try to protect the powerless.

00:33:25.200 --> 00:33:30.200
Now, how do the changes
that we've seen affect...

00:33:31.170 --> 00:33:32.380
The question was
particularly about health.

00:33:32.380 --> 00:33:35.200
I think there are two
things to say about that,

00:33:35.200 --> 00:33:38.864
one is back to don't throw stones

00:33:38.864 --> 00:33:40.550
if you're in a glass house.

00:33:40.550 --> 00:33:42.551
I mean, iliberalism I
don't need to tell you

00:33:42.551 --> 00:33:44.950
the attack on rules-based systems

00:33:44.950 --> 00:33:49.950
I mean, I work in New York
but I'm British citizen,

00:33:50.360 --> 00:33:51.940
both of our countries
have suffered attacks

00:33:51.940 --> 00:33:54.950
on their own rule of law recently

00:33:55.900 --> 00:33:57.570
and that is a trend

00:33:57.570 --> 00:34:01.082
across the industrialized democratic world

00:34:01.082 --> 00:34:06.082
that the rules-based order
is being poorly defended

00:34:06.190 --> 00:34:08.790
by the world's democratic states,

00:34:08.790 --> 00:34:11.060
which are a minority of states

00:34:12.040 --> 00:34:15.450
and certainly and a small
minority of the global population

00:34:15.450 --> 00:34:18.010
live in fully democratic societies.

00:34:18.010 --> 00:34:20.020
And Fareed Zakaria wrote 20 years ago

00:34:20.020 --> 00:34:22.870
about the rise of illiberal democracy.

00:34:22.870 --> 00:34:25.277
So we've gotta tend to our own houses

00:34:25.277 --> 00:34:29.220
and actually just in parenthesis,

00:34:29.220 --> 00:34:30.660
the Biden administration's approach

00:34:30.660 --> 00:34:33.140
to the Russia-Ukraine crisis

00:34:33.140 --> 00:34:36.810
has actually been an assertion
of democratic strength

00:34:36.810 --> 00:34:38.900
in the face of an assault
on the rules-based order,

00:34:38.900 --> 00:34:41.633
by a leading autocratic country.

00:34:42.700 --> 00:34:43.770
The second thing though,

00:34:43.770 --> 00:34:47.780
is where and how to combat the assault

00:34:47.780 --> 00:34:48.750
on the rules-based order

00:34:48.750 --> 00:34:51.773
from those who don't want
to live by rules at home,

00:34:52.978 --> 00:34:55.340
in the autocratic world.

00:34:55.340 --> 00:34:57.720
And here there's a real push and pull

00:34:57.720 --> 00:34:59.160
because on the one hand,

00:34:59.160 --> 00:35:01.750
the Chinese leadership fear
instability on the other hand,

00:35:01.750 --> 00:35:04.290
they don't want to have
their own power curbed

00:35:04.290 --> 00:35:06.770
and I think this speaks to very much

00:35:06.770 --> 00:35:11.770
the agenda of the liberal democratic world

00:35:14.370 --> 00:35:16.970
standing together in a very clear way,

00:35:16.970 --> 00:35:19.580
not to invent new rules,
but to defend old rules

00:35:19.580 --> 00:35:21.340
that all countries have signed

00:35:21.340 --> 00:35:22.620
because by signing the UN Charter,

00:35:22.620 --> 00:35:24.180
all countries have signed up to

00:35:24.180 --> 00:35:28.042
a basic set of human rights
and that has to be fought over

00:35:28.042 --> 00:35:30.438
and that's about politics in the end.

00:35:30.438 --> 00:35:32.690
- And I think I'm glad
that you went there,

00:35:32.690 --> 00:35:35.300
you went to not just sort of people

00:35:35.300 --> 00:35:37.890
not playing by the rules if you will

00:35:37.890 --> 00:35:42.890
at the interstate level,
but sort of between states

00:35:45.220 --> 00:35:46.890
in terms of between state dynamics,

00:35:46.890 --> 00:35:51.890
but also domestically and
that is frankly something

00:35:51.900 --> 00:35:54.942
that worries some of us a little bit more,

00:35:54.942 --> 00:35:59.760
the things that you took
for grant are under threat.

00:35:59.760 --> 00:36:03.290
And so extending that
conversation a little bit,

00:36:03.290 --> 00:36:05.220
because there are actors,

00:36:05.220 --> 00:36:07.500
both state-based and non-state actors

00:36:08.457 --> 00:36:11.780
that now in this interconnected world

00:36:11.780 --> 00:36:15.408
that has the power for major disruption

00:36:15.408 --> 00:36:19.830
and as I understand, ICRC recently saw

00:36:21.390 --> 00:36:26.390
in terms of breach of
data of vulnerable people

00:36:27.597 --> 00:36:30.900
and I understand that coming
from a question recently

00:36:30.900 --> 00:36:32.190
and you feel free to correct me

00:36:32.190 --> 00:36:34.880
if this information is inaccurate

00:36:34.880 --> 00:36:38.353
by a malicious cyber actor,

00:36:39.570 --> 00:36:41.910
what does the humanitarian
sector need to do?

00:36:41.910 --> 00:36:43.963
- Oh, that's interesting.

00:36:43.963 --> 00:36:45.560
ICRC, just so everyone knows

00:36:45.560 --> 00:36:46.393
is the international committee on the-

00:36:46.393 --> 00:36:48.146
- Yeah, no, not you guys.

00:36:48.146 --> 00:36:50.420
Not you guys.
- Not us guys (chuckles).

00:36:50.420 --> 00:36:52.140
if you're a donor don't worry,

00:36:52.140 --> 00:36:53.910
we're keeping your stuff safe.
- Yeah, no.

00:36:53.910 --> 00:36:54.743
- Now, let just say one thing about that-

00:36:54.743 --> 00:36:56.690
- So the question came
for the broader sector.

00:36:56.690 --> 00:36:58.291
- Yeah, I understand, here's
a really interesting point-

00:36:58.291 --> 00:36:59.140
- Yeah.

00:36:59.140 --> 00:37:00.610
- All the pressure in our sectors

00:37:00.610 --> 00:37:03.428
to spend less on "administration."

00:37:03.428 --> 00:37:04.261
- Yeah.

00:37:04.261 --> 00:37:07.133
- But good administration
means protecting data

00:37:07.133 --> 00:37:09.770
of clients as well as supporters

00:37:09.770 --> 00:37:11.020
and you gotta spend on that.

00:37:11.020 --> 00:37:14.140
Spending on cybersecurity
is almost bad spending

00:37:14.140 --> 00:37:16.010
in the way some of the charity watchdogs

00:37:16.010 --> 00:37:17.893
and others work on this.

00:37:18.747 --> 00:37:20.020
Any organization that says

00:37:20.020 --> 00:37:22.290
it doesn't spend anything
on administration

00:37:22.290 --> 00:37:24.850
means it's not spending
anything on cybersecurity

00:37:24.850 --> 00:37:25.880
and that can't be sensible.

00:37:25.880 --> 00:37:28.800
So, I could answer the
question at great length,

00:37:28.800 --> 00:37:31.666
but I think just the most
basic thing to say is,

00:37:31.666 --> 00:37:34.085
what's the responsibility
of the humanitarian sector,

00:37:34.085 --> 00:37:37.040
is the responsibility to tell
the truth that this costs

00:37:37.040 --> 00:37:38.390
and it's a good investment.

00:37:39.461 --> 00:37:44.150
- Yeah, no I agree and not
all administrative costs

00:37:46.584 --> 00:37:47.860
are created equal.

00:37:47.860 --> 00:37:50.630
Sometimes what is charge
at administrative costs

00:37:51.600 --> 00:37:55.070
increases the efficiency
of the direct costs

00:37:55.070 --> 00:37:56.840
of individual projects and initiatives

00:37:56.840 --> 00:37:59.475
and I think all of us
need to do a better job,

00:37:59.475 --> 00:38:02.640
including academic to communicate that,

00:38:02.640 --> 00:38:07.640
that this money can be very well utilized

00:38:08.538 --> 00:38:12.010
and sort of mitigate a
lot of substantial risks.

00:38:12.010 --> 00:38:14.290
And so there's this interesting question,

00:38:14.290 --> 00:38:16.060
at least I find it interesting

00:38:16.060 --> 00:38:18.168
I think from a postdoctoral
fellow who has a small gun-

00:38:18.168 --> 00:38:22.043
- Oh my God, there's no way
I can match this brain power.

00:38:23.330 --> 00:38:25.576
- So, she talks about a small grant

00:38:25.576 --> 00:38:29.980
she got to aid the general
health of Afghan people

00:38:29.980 --> 00:38:32.821
recently resettled in Oklahoma City.

00:38:32.821 --> 00:38:34.410
And if you had to...

00:38:34.410 --> 00:38:36.010
She's taking an opportunity to ask you,

00:38:36.010 --> 00:38:38.514
if you had to she has a small grant

00:38:38.514 --> 00:38:42.321
and has a sort of a focus area,

00:38:42.321 --> 00:38:45.657
but some flexibility
to where to spend this

00:38:45.657 --> 00:38:46.890
and how to spend this.

00:38:46.890 --> 00:38:50.796
If you had to pick one
health problem to focus on

00:38:50.796 --> 00:38:55.689
obviously difficult because
there a lot of things that...

00:38:55.689 --> 00:38:57.105
This is a difficult question

00:38:57.105 --> 00:39:00.457
because there are a lot of
things that are important,

00:39:00.457 --> 00:39:04.740
would you go to the psychosocial
and mental health promotion

00:39:04.740 --> 00:39:06.400
or a physical health intervention

00:39:07.350 --> 00:39:10.960
in Afghan refugee population
in this moment in time

00:39:10.960 --> 00:39:12.670
in an American city?

00:39:12.670 --> 00:39:14.980
- Well, we don't actually
work in Oklahoma,

00:39:14.980 --> 00:39:16.090
but we work across the country.

00:39:16.090 --> 00:39:19.500
I'll tell you what I would
guess we would spend it on

00:39:19.500 --> 00:39:21.750
and then I'll explain
why I use the word guess.

00:39:21.750 --> 00:39:26.750
I would guess we would decide
to spend it on mental health,

00:39:28.060 --> 00:39:30.640
because I know that significant numbers

00:39:30.640 --> 00:39:33.600
of the Afghan arrivals
are deeply traumatized

00:39:33.600 --> 00:39:36.720
by their survival and the fact
they've left people behind.

00:39:36.720 --> 00:39:38.560
The reason I say guess is very important,

00:39:38.560 --> 00:39:39.657
because the right answer if I may say so,

00:39:39.657 --> 00:39:42.850
so the question is, I'd ask the clients,

00:39:42.850 --> 00:39:45.160
I mean, it's a big part of our approach

00:39:45.160 --> 00:39:49.100
that our needs assessments are
not done by people like me,

00:39:49.100 --> 00:39:51.330
they're done by human-centered designers

00:39:51.330 --> 00:39:53.530
who are talking to clients
and assessing need.

00:39:53.530 --> 00:39:57.240
And so I hope that the grant
giver who are question...

00:39:57.240 --> 00:39:59.510
Obviously, I hope they'll
give us the grant,

00:39:59.510 --> 00:40:01.100
although in Oklahoma I suppose they can't.

00:40:01.100 --> 00:40:03.330
But the best answer I think,

00:40:03.330 --> 00:40:05.620
surely is to say, you'd
have to ask the people

00:40:05.620 --> 00:40:07.460
and test with the people.

00:40:07.460 --> 00:40:11.172
My instinct is that the proximate need

00:40:11.172 --> 00:40:15.673
is more likely to be greater
on the mental health side

00:40:15.673 --> 00:40:17.290
than on the physical health side,

00:40:17.290 --> 00:40:19.520
but I'd want to cover myself by saying

00:40:19.520 --> 00:40:21.960
that's only a guess and
I'd want to really test it

00:40:21.960 --> 00:40:23.605
with the population.

00:40:23.605 --> 00:40:26.090
- Interesting, so the next...

00:40:26.090 --> 00:40:28.950
And I think it's in line
with sort of the ethos

00:40:28.950 --> 00:40:30.990
of a community-based public health,

00:40:30.990 --> 00:40:33.540
you ask the population, you do the work

00:40:34.800 --> 00:40:37.360
using tools like (indistinct) and others.

00:40:37.360 --> 00:40:39.880
And so the next question
actually comes from

00:40:40.830 --> 00:40:43.513
the Dean of our School of
Public Health here at Yale,

00:40:44.383 --> 00:40:47.540
Sten Vermund and his question
is that IRC is famous,

00:40:47.540 --> 00:40:50.670
very famous for its region to regions

00:40:50.670 --> 00:40:52.163
of the most acute need.

00:40:53.940 --> 00:40:57.567
And what are the strategies
that you're using now,

00:40:58.944 --> 00:41:03.230
in supporting and rebuilding
healthcare systems

00:41:03.230 --> 00:41:05.780
when political systems
are very unsupported.

00:41:05.780 --> 00:41:10.780
So for example, Syria,
Myanmar or somewhat paralyzed

00:41:10.970 --> 00:41:13.450
like Haiti, Yemen, and Congo,

00:41:13.450 --> 00:41:15.770
where the intention perhaps at least

00:41:15.770 --> 00:41:19.870
a lot of the intention is there,
but capability is not there

00:41:19.870 --> 00:41:23.210
because public health
101 is get the buy in

00:41:23.210 --> 00:41:27.390
from the government and
the governed, et cetera,

00:41:27.390 --> 00:41:28.680
but when you don't have that

00:41:28.680 --> 00:41:30.010
and sometimes they're antagonistic,

00:41:30.010 --> 00:41:33.033
so how do you solve that conundrum?

00:41:34.150 --> 00:41:37.299
- Well, we do health
system strengthening work

00:41:37.299 --> 00:41:39.690
in partnership with local governments

00:41:39.690 --> 00:41:40.523
or national governments

00:41:40.523 --> 00:41:42.380
where they are willing to work with us.

00:41:43.950 --> 00:41:47.513
But in places like Northwest
Syria or elsewhere,

00:41:48.380 --> 00:41:51.240
the national government is oppositional

00:41:51.240 --> 00:41:54.642
and the local authorities are non-state

00:41:54.642 --> 00:41:59.642
and so we're in exactly the bind,

00:42:00.201 --> 00:42:02.450
the Dean of your school and
he's probably got a better

00:42:02.450 --> 00:42:04.320
or she's got a better
answer than I have to this.

00:42:04.320 --> 00:42:06.820
But the kind of things I
would talk about are one,

00:42:10.010 --> 00:42:12.800
the information on war really matters

00:42:12.800 --> 00:42:15.520
and disinformation and misinformation

00:42:15.520 --> 00:42:18.400
is the first enemy of
effective public health.

00:42:18.400 --> 00:42:23.100
And we as trusted partners
of local communities

00:42:23.100 --> 00:42:24.670
we can work well

00:42:24.670 --> 00:42:27.260
in tackling misinformation
and disinformation.

00:42:27.260 --> 00:42:32.240
Secondly, you will know better than I

00:42:32.240 --> 00:42:35.700
that a lot of community-based care

00:42:35.700 --> 00:42:38.420
can achieve far more per dollar

00:42:38.420 --> 00:42:40.970
than health center or hospital-based care.

00:42:40.970 --> 00:42:43.770
So the second thing we've got four

00:42:43.770 --> 00:42:45.040
research and innovation priorities

00:42:45.040 --> 00:42:46.710
one is about sexual and
reproductive health,

00:42:46.710 --> 00:42:48.230
maternal and newborn health

00:42:48.230 --> 00:42:50.333
and it's very much focused on self care.

00:42:51.280 --> 00:42:54.480
We have a second research
and innovation priority

00:42:54.480 --> 00:42:56.030
around malnutrition,

00:42:56.030 --> 00:42:58.980
the current system for a
severe acute malnutrition

00:42:58.980 --> 00:43:00.770
is separate from moderate,
acute malnutrition

00:43:00.770 --> 00:43:03.380
and it depends on people
getting to health centers,

00:43:03.380 --> 00:43:06.440
which in South Sudan, they're
not gonna make it there.

00:43:06.440 --> 00:43:09.480
So again, you build from
the community level,

00:43:09.480 --> 00:43:14.480
the diagnosis and
treatment at a local level.

00:43:14.640 --> 00:43:17.800
Thirdly, an important part of our work now

00:43:17.800 --> 00:43:19.670
is to work with non-state partners

00:43:19.670 --> 00:43:21.180
as well as state partners,

00:43:21.180 --> 00:43:22.630
community based organizations,

00:43:22.630 --> 00:43:24.730
that's an important way of winning trust,

00:43:24.730 --> 00:43:26.910
but also extending scale.

00:43:26.910 --> 00:43:29.660
Now that doesn't quite count
as building a health system,

00:43:29.660 --> 00:43:30.790
which was the Dean's answer,

00:43:30.790 --> 00:43:34.720
I dunno whether you can mic him or her up

00:43:36.744 --> 00:43:38.380
to say what would be a better answer,

00:43:38.380 --> 00:43:42.810
because I'm obviously far less
of an expert than him or her.

00:43:42.810 --> 00:43:45.950
But those are the kind of
things that we are trying to do

00:43:45.950 --> 00:43:48.594
to sustain a web of health interventions

00:43:48.594 --> 00:43:50.840
below the secondary care level,

00:43:50.840 --> 00:43:53.683
which is frankly not where we do the work.

00:43:54.580 --> 00:43:58.610
- Wonderful, so let's see if we can add

00:43:58.610 --> 00:44:03.563
Sten to the panel and if he is
able to join from that side.

00:44:05.160 --> 00:44:06.270
One thing that you might wanna

00:44:06.270 --> 00:44:08.840
and I wanted to keep that for the end,

00:44:08.840 --> 00:44:09.733
but I don't wanna forget about it

00:44:09.733 --> 00:44:11.880
because we have a lot of questions.

00:44:11.880 --> 00:44:14.047
So you'd love to hear
this, one of the comments,

00:44:14.047 --> 00:44:15.870
it says not a question,

00:44:15.870 --> 00:44:18.190
but I do wanna say thank you to IRC

00:44:18.190 --> 00:44:19.960
for doing an amazing job

00:44:19.960 --> 00:44:24.100
and I thought of just
a blanket appreciation

00:44:24.100 --> 00:44:26.869
because this person is a
former refugee from Rwanda

00:44:26.869 --> 00:44:29.033
and with support from IRC,

00:44:30.980 --> 00:44:35.980
they were able to restart their
life here in the US in 2000

00:44:37.290 --> 00:44:39.280
and they're now soon to be a student

00:44:39.280 --> 00:44:40.463
at Yale School of Public Health.

00:44:40.463 --> 00:44:42.066
- Wow, that's fantastic.

00:44:42.066 --> 00:44:44.020
- Isn't that gratifying?

00:44:44.020 --> 00:44:46.110
- That's fantastic, really heartwarming

00:44:46.110 --> 00:44:50.780
and if he or she writes to
me, sends to me an email

00:44:50.780 --> 00:44:53.040
I'd love to find out about their story.

00:44:53.040 --> 00:44:56.810
I mean, look I think
we are now the large...

00:44:56.810 --> 00:44:59.890
I think I've said this, the
largest impact evaluation agency

00:44:59.890 --> 00:45:00.820
in the humanitarian sector

00:45:00.820 --> 00:45:03.450
and that's 'cause we go
to places that people

00:45:03.450 --> 00:45:06.490
aren't used to studying what really works,

00:45:06.490 --> 00:45:09.670
but we think it matters even
more in emergency situations

00:45:09.670 --> 00:45:13.130
to use the money well and
to use right approaches

00:45:13.130 --> 00:45:15.930
and when you hear a story

00:45:15.930 --> 00:45:20.293
like the one I you you've just
said, that's really great.

00:45:21.480 --> 00:45:22.510
- Wonderful.

00:45:22.510 --> 00:45:26.600
So I got a message that
says Sten has been promoted

00:45:26.600 --> 00:45:28.550
and when faculty hear this news,

00:45:28.550 --> 00:45:31.760
it usually whether they got
tenure or became full professor,

00:45:31.760 --> 00:45:36.400
but Sten is already a seasoned
public health professional

00:45:36.400 --> 00:45:38.690
this essentially means that he's here

00:45:38.690 --> 00:45:40.730
on the Zoom panel side.

00:45:40.730 --> 00:45:44.070
So Sten, do you wanna sort
of add to what David said?

00:45:44.070 --> 00:45:46.283
- Give a better answer than I gave?

00:45:47.260 --> 00:45:51.120
- Well, thank you very much,
I wasn't expecting to speak

00:45:51.120 --> 00:45:55.660
but David your response about the pivoting

00:45:55.660 --> 00:45:59.830
from national partners to local partners

00:45:59.830 --> 00:46:01.750
and community partners,

00:46:01.750 --> 00:46:04.990
I think that is very insightful

00:46:04.990 --> 00:46:06.610
and really gets to the point

00:46:06.610 --> 00:46:09.270
that I was thinking you might make

00:46:09.270 --> 00:46:14.270
because I've connected to
IRC even as a young man

00:46:16.060 --> 00:46:20.780
with some of the nature of
the work of your organization

00:46:20.780 --> 00:46:25.220
and I feel that when we have circumstances

00:46:25.220 --> 00:46:29.793
that might look to professionals or donors

00:46:29.793 --> 00:46:32.993
or purses of goodwill as almost hopeless.

00:46:33.840 --> 00:46:38.773
The Rohingya in central Myanmar
driven from their homes,

00:46:39.690 --> 00:46:44.690
Syria, circumstances in Eastern Congo,

00:46:46.490 --> 00:46:51.490
I do think that your answer
is precisely what is required.

00:46:52.210 --> 00:46:57.210
One has to pivot to local
players and stakeholders

00:46:59.220 --> 00:47:04.220
and see how one can
reinforce the circumstances

00:47:05.720 --> 00:47:09.730
that maybe without government support

00:47:09.730 --> 00:47:13.410
or without long term sustainability,

00:47:13.410 --> 00:47:17.010
just to tied communities over

00:47:17.010 --> 00:47:20.960
at a time of tremendous crisis and stress.

00:47:20.960 --> 00:47:23.570
So I've seen you do that,

00:47:23.570 --> 00:47:25.740
so I wasn't surprised by your response

00:47:25.740 --> 00:47:27.060
and quite the contrary,

00:47:27.060 --> 00:47:30.070
I thought it was an inspiring response.

00:47:30.070 --> 00:47:31.020
Thank you.
- Thank you.

00:47:31.020 --> 00:47:33.210
- Yeah, and often like

00:47:33.210 --> 00:47:35.470
and I'm sure you have the same experience

00:47:35.470 --> 00:47:36.930
that often when you work

00:47:36.930 --> 00:47:40.070
with community-based
organizations in weak states

00:47:40.070 --> 00:47:43.868
or where the governance
systems are not that robust

00:47:43.868 --> 00:47:47.500
when things stabilize
often it's the manpower

00:47:47.500 --> 00:47:49.560
from these community-based organizations

00:47:49.560 --> 00:47:52.910
and sometimes not always
it's their legitimization,

00:47:52.910 --> 00:47:54.230
especially if the resolution

00:47:54.230 --> 00:47:59.230
is towards the better state
of things in these situations.

00:48:00.310 --> 00:48:05.310
And so again, it's not necessarily
bypassing the government,

00:48:05.700 --> 00:48:09.057
it is leveraging what is available

00:48:09.057 --> 00:48:11.790
and often community-based organizations,

00:48:11.790 --> 00:48:14.334
if I may say, is a mechanism
for self-governance

00:48:14.334 --> 00:48:16.780
that some communities use

00:48:16.780 --> 00:48:21.780
to fill in some of those
gaps in services, et cetera.

00:48:23.600 --> 00:48:26.428
And so there's this interesting,

00:48:26.428 --> 00:48:31.428
but provocative, if I may
say so, question around,

00:48:31.470 --> 00:48:34.570
what is the role or approach of the IRC

00:48:34.570 --> 00:48:38.328
in challenging powers, such
as nations with veto powers

00:48:38.328 --> 00:48:41.036
who are seemingly immune from sanctions

00:48:41.036 --> 00:48:44.030
and other measures that you mentioned

00:48:44.030 --> 00:48:46.230
in situations such as the crisis in Yemen.

00:48:46.230 --> 00:48:49.490
But I would add so the
question was around Yemen,

00:48:49.490 --> 00:48:52.940
but I would say there's
more than one situation

00:48:56.219 --> 00:48:57.450
where this is the case.

00:48:58.430 --> 00:49:01.200
- Well, look, I think our
role is always twofold,

00:49:01.200 --> 00:49:02.033
one is to serve the people

00:49:02.033 --> 00:49:05.046
'cause we have to abide by
the humanitarian principles

00:49:05.046 --> 00:49:09.140
of impartiality independence
and the humanity

00:49:09.140 --> 00:49:12.653
is very, very important,
neutrality as well.

00:49:13.600 --> 00:49:16.343
But second, I think we
have to bear witness

00:49:16.343 --> 00:49:19.200
and we have to speak about
what's actually happening

00:49:19.200 --> 00:49:21.270
and if there are allies of the US

00:49:21.270 --> 00:49:24.640
who are interfering in humanitarian
aid, we gotta say that.

00:49:24.640 --> 00:49:27.060
Now, I'll be absolutely honest with you

00:49:27.060 --> 00:49:28.400
without going into specifics,

00:49:28.400 --> 00:49:29.410
there are places in the world

00:49:29.410 --> 00:49:31.470
where it's too dangerous
for us to speak out

00:49:31.470 --> 00:49:33.020
and that is a real danger.

00:49:33.020 --> 00:49:36.190
In this emergency watch
list that I talked about,

00:49:36.190 --> 00:49:37.530
that we launched for this year

00:49:37.530 --> 00:49:38.962
and launched in December in a lecture

00:49:38.962 --> 00:49:40.810
at the Council of Foreign Relations,

00:49:40.810 --> 00:49:41.800
I made the case that we need

00:49:41.800 --> 00:49:44.640
an independent office for the protection

00:49:44.640 --> 00:49:48.441
of humanitarian access
because UN officials and NGOs

00:49:48.441 --> 00:49:49.950
are in double jeopardy.

00:49:49.950 --> 00:49:52.120
If we speak out, we can get kicked out

00:49:52.120 --> 00:49:55.670
and we need some independent leadership

00:49:55.670 --> 00:49:56.930
and some independent...

00:49:56.930 --> 00:49:58.330
We could supply the information

00:49:58.330 --> 00:49:59.300
but then it would be anonymized

00:49:59.300 --> 00:50:01.643
so that it could then be used.

00:50:02.530 --> 00:50:04.530
But I think those are
our two responsibilities,

00:50:04.530 --> 00:50:05.440
we've gotta serve the people,

00:50:05.440 --> 00:50:07.030
but we've also gotta speak,

00:50:07.030 --> 00:50:10.100
not for them, but we've
gotta speak to what we see

00:50:10.100 --> 00:50:11.890
and that's what we try to do.

00:50:11.890 --> 00:50:13.070
I mean, we're doing
that on our Afghanistan,

00:50:13.070 --> 00:50:16.070
I mean, we are very, very, very negative

00:50:16.070 --> 00:50:18.720
about the current
international economic policy

00:50:18.720 --> 00:50:20.539
towards Afghanistan,

00:50:20.539 --> 00:50:24.820
even though we're a partner
of the US government in the US

00:50:24.820 --> 00:50:27.573
and happy to partner
with them on the ground.

00:50:29.070 --> 00:50:31.130
- Yeah, no, no, I think
that is the approach

00:50:31.130 --> 00:50:32.780
that's how you thread the needle.

00:50:35.478 --> 00:50:39.330
So I have two questions
and two final questions

00:50:39.330 --> 00:50:42.880
then ultimate and the final
questions, related questions.

00:50:42.880 --> 00:50:45.529
One from a student and the
other one from a faculty member

00:50:45.529 --> 00:50:48.170
and you alluded to some part of it,

00:50:48.170 --> 00:50:49.920
but I'll start with the student question

00:50:49.920 --> 00:50:51.470
and you can take them together.

00:50:52.410 --> 00:50:54.160
The question is many
in our audiences aspire

00:50:54.160 --> 00:50:55.640
to be global health professionals

00:50:55.640 --> 00:50:58.373
focused on humanitarian crises,

00:50:59.240 --> 00:51:03.161
what advice can you share
for these individuals

00:51:03.161 --> 00:51:05.410
that could inspire them?

00:51:05.410 --> 00:51:08.220
And the second one is again related,

00:51:08.220 --> 00:51:11.600
you mentioned that you are
the biggest impact evaluator,

00:51:11.600 --> 00:51:13.160
which is very valuable

00:51:13.160 --> 00:51:16.130
and sometimes knowing what
doesn't work is equally important

00:51:16.130 --> 00:51:19.053
as figuring out what works.

00:51:22.790 --> 00:51:25.980
You face a lot of difficult
challenges like this,

00:51:25.980 --> 00:51:28.250
how does IRC and other
humanitarian agencies

00:51:28.250 --> 00:51:32.980
prepare their staff to
face ethical challenges

00:51:32.980 --> 00:51:35.320
and how do they partner with universities

00:51:36.430 --> 00:51:41.430
in enhancing some of these
actions and capabilities

00:51:42.550 --> 00:51:44.952
where both the partners can benefit

00:51:44.952 --> 00:51:46.543
in terms of their mission?

00:51:47.511 --> 00:51:49.720
- Right, I mean, on the first I think,

00:51:49.720 --> 00:51:51.160
apply for jobs at the IRC.

00:51:51.160 --> 00:51:54.580
I mean, most of the
jobs are for local hires

00:51:54.580 --> 00:51:55.640
in the places that we work,

00:51:55.640 --> 00:51:59.053
but we do have global hires as well,

00:52:00.540 --> 00:52:03.120
but I think the best advice
is to get your boots dirty,

00:52:03.120 --> 00:52:05.150
I wish I'd done that
when I was in my twenties

00:52:05.150 --> 00:52:06.220
and spend time interna...

00:52:06.220 --> 00:52:09.114
I mean, I spent time at
MIT that doesn't count

00:52:09.114 --> 00:52:14.114
as international experience
really as a graduate student.

00:52:15.890 --> 00:52:19.063
So I think get your boots
dirty on the ground,

00:52:21.950 --> 00:52:25.183
doing research or seeing what works,

00:52:26.412 --> 00:52:29.630
because I think that's
invaluable and your twenties

00:52:29.630 --> 00:52:31.300
is the right out time to do it.

00:52:31.300 --> 00:52:33.133
Probably your twenties and your sixties.

00:52:34.930 --> 00:52:36.630
In respect to the second question,

00:52:36.630 --> 00:52:38.800
I'm maybe not gonna answer
it as well as I should.

00:52:38.800 --> 00:52:41.570
First of all, we have our
own ethical standards boards

00:52:41.570 --> 00:52:43.850
and we have to sustain
our own ethical standards

00:52:43.850 --> 00:52:44.700
that's important.

00:52:46.296 --> 00:52:48.230
We don't do randomized control trials

00:52:48.230 --> 00:52:52.400
of denying some people food
and giving other people food

00:52:52.400 --> 00:52:54.750
and seeing what the consequences.

00:52:54.750 --> 00:52:55.583
So we have to...

00:52:55.583 --> 00:52:58.190
I mean, maybe that's a
too obvious an example,

00:52:58.190 --> 00:53:02.520
but we have process to try
and adhere to high standards.

00:53:02.520 --> 00:53:04.693
I think that my appeal
to you, would be that...

00:53:04.693 --> 00:53:07.700
I mean, this really befuddles
me, I have to confess to you,

00:53:07.700 --> 00:53:08.820
I know this is a public event,

00:53:08.820 --> 00:53:12.493
so maybe, I'll think how I say this,

00:53:12.493 --> 00:53:17.493
but I'm completely flummoxed
that a 41 billion sector

00:53:18.240 --> 00:53:21.440
of the global economy finds
it so difficult to spend money

00:53:21.440 --> 00:53:24.610
on evidence making it's completely crazy.

00:53:24.610 --> 00:53:27.000
I mean, I thought eight years
ago when I joined the IRC

00:53:27.000 --> 00:53:29.620
that we made outcomes,

00:53:29.620 --> 00:53:31.850
evidence value for money and innovation,

00:53:31.850 --> 00:53:35.090
RND our sort of washing line

00:53:35.090 --> 00:53:37.855
or the spine is a better way
of putting it of our effort.

00:53:37.855 --> 00:53:39.000
And I thought, well,

00:53:39.000 --> 00:53:41.513
it's obvious at the time it
was $26 billion of spent,

00:53:41.513 --> 00:53:44.658
I thought, well, of course
if I was a minister still,

00:53:44.658 --> 00:53:47.610
first thing I'd do is say to the taxpayer,

00:53:47.610 --> 00:53:48.700
well, sometimes we get things right

00:53:48.700 --> 00:53:49.550
sometimes we get things wrong,

00:53:49.550 --> 00:53:52.140
but we always put our money
behind what we think works

00:53:52.140 --> 00:53:53.832
and what we think works
is not just by sticking

00:53:53.832 --> 00:53:55.380
our finger in the air,

00:53:55.380 --> 00:53:57.290
it's by doing proper evidence making

00:53:57.290 --> 00:53:58.930
and if it then transpires

00:53:58.930 --> 00:54:00.830
that the evidence shows it doesn't work,

00:54:00.830 --> 00:54:02.230
well, we were following the evidence

00:54:02.230 --> 00:54:03.770
and then it got updated.

00:54:03.770 --> 00:54:04.980
It's very, very hard

00:54:04.980 --> 00:54:07.480
to raise the for evidence
making, it's crazy

00:54:07.480 --> 00:54:11.790
and so if you want a good thing
to come out of this seminar,

00:54:11.790 --> 00:54:15.100
it would be a way for Yale
School of Public health and us

00:54:16.410 --> 00:54:19.080
to raise a fund, to do
proper evidence making.

00:54:19.080 --> 00:54:21.540
Because that's still short changed

00:54:21.540 --> 00:54:23.570
in a completely shortsighted way

00:54:23.570 --> 00:54:28.570
and it's absurd really
for our leadership team

00:54:28.680 --> 00:54:30.450
to be presented with the question,

00:54:30.450 --> 00:54:31.760
how much you put into evidence making

00:54:31.760 --> 00:54:34.480
versus how much you put into programs.

00:54:34.480 --> 00:54:39.480
I mean, that's a obvious
choice of really a bad kind

00:54:39.620 --> 00:54:41.880
and I think that would be a
good thing to come out of this.

00:54:41.880 --> 00:54:43.827
- Absolutely data is not distraction.

